<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vratislaus II of Bohemia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1032–<a href="/years/1092">1092</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Vratislav II · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/premyslid-dynasty">Přemyslid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vratislaus II was Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> from 1061 and the first Bohemian ruler to hold the title of king, granted personally by Emperor Henry IV in 1085 for his support during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/investiture-controversy">Investiture Controversy</a>. He moved his residence to Vyšehrad and founded a chapter there. The royal title was not hereditary and lapsed at his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vratislaus-ii-of-bohemia.jpg" alt="Vratislaus II of Bohemia" width="320" height="514" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous fresco in Znojmo (CZ) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vratislav_II.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vratislaus II was the son of Bretislaus I and Judith of Schweinfurt. When his father died in 1055 he became duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olomouc</a>, while his older brother became Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> as Spytihněv II. The two fell out and Vratislaus was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. With Hungarian assistance he regained Olomouc, was reconciled with his brother and succeeded him as duke of Bohemia when he died in 1061. His younger brothers Conrad and Otto held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a> and Olomouc, following the Přemyslid tradition of entrusting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> to younger brothers, and the youngest, Jaromír, entered the church.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Popes Alexander II and Gregory VII confirmed Vratislaus in the privilege of wearing the mitre and tunic, yet he supported Emperor Henry IV in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/investiture-controversy">Investiture Controversy</a> and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> rebellions. He joined Henry in 1075 and they defeated the rebels at the First Battle of Langensalza on 9 June. At Flarchheim his contingent enabled the imperial army to overcome the rebels of Rudolf of Rheinfelden, and he seized Rudolf's golden lance, which was afterwards carried before him on state occasions. He won again at Mailberg in 1082, and in thanks Henry granted him the title of king in 1085.</p><p>He often quarrelled with his brother Jaromír, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>. In 1063 Vratislaus founded a diocese at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olomouc</a>, which Jaromír ignored, and in 1070 he created the Vyšehrad Chapter, endowed independently of the Prague bishop. In 1080 he asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> for permission to use the Slavonic liturgy and was refused. He joined Henry's Italian campaign of 1081 and entered Rome with him in 1083. He held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-lusatia">Lower Lusatia</a> from 1075 to 1086 but never received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a> as promised, and after 1088 he gave Henry no military aid, though he stayed loyal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His last years were taken up with dynastic quarrels. When Otto died in 1086 Vratislaus gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olomouc</a> to his son Boleslaus, which Conrad took as an act against his interests. The army sent against Conrad under another son, Bretislaus, turned on him. Vratislaus named Conrad his heir, and together they attacked Bretislaus, who fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. Vratislaus died of a hunting wound on 14 January 1092 and was buried in the Church of St Peter and Paul at Vyšehrad. His reduction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> bishop's power strengthened the Bohemian crown, and his policy towards the Empire was followed in the 12th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vratislaus II of Bohemia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Two earlier dukes had received a personal royal title from the emperor, <b>Vratislaus II</b> in 1085 and Vladislaus II in 1158, but neither could pass it on.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Vratislaus II</b> received a personal royal title in 1085 and Vladislaus II in 1158.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bohemia">Duchy of Bohemia</a></li><li><blockquote>Olomouc in central Moravia went to <b>Vratislaus</b>, then in 1061 to Otto I the Fair, whose line held it through the 12th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-olomouc">Duchy of Olomouc</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.83,14.33&amp;year=1071" aria-label="Bohemia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Vratislaus II of Bohemia"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5782 5780h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5782 5780h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=49.83,14.33&amp;year=1071">What was here in 1071? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
